WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.720
The cochlear amplifier helps us hear weak sounds, separate nearby frequencies, and

2
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.480
handle a wide range of loudness.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.480
It is an active process.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.080
In mammals, outer hair cells add energy to the vibration on each cycle of a sound.

5
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.880
Their motor is a protein called prestin.

6
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.260
A microphone in the ear canal can pick up sound that the cochlea sends back out.

7
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.800
That sound gives us a way to check outer hair cell function.

8
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.800
For more information visit www .preston .com

9
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:32.463
How does the cell add energy?

10
00:00:33.603 --> 00:00:37.383
Current through the transduction channel sets the voltage that drives prestin.

11
00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:40.623
Outer hair cells shorten and lengthen with voltage.

12
00:00:41.603 --> 00:00:45.103
Prestin responds directly, without burning ATP each cycle.

13
00:00:45.963 --> 00:00:50.323
In a prestin knock-in mouse, transduction and cell mechanics were normal, yet

14
00:00:50.323 --> 00:00:51.723
amplification was lost.

15
00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:57.043
That is evidence that the cell body's motility is necessary for mammalian

16
00:00:57.043 --> 00:00:58.043
amplification.

17
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:02.417
One electrical signature is nonlinear capacitance.

18
00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:07.757
In whole-cell voltage clamp, a cell expressing prestin shows a bell-shaped

19
00:01:07.757 --> 00:01:08.817
capacitance curve.

20
00:01:09.657 --> 00:01:13.457
The usual reading is voltage-dependent charge movement in the protein.

21
00:01:14.297 --> 00:01:17.317
It does not measure the force or power a hair cell produces.

22
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.370
Now listen to the returning sound.

23
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.170
Otoacoustic emissions depend on the active process.

24
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.810
A microphone in the sealed ear canal records them.

25
00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.950
These tests are used in newborn hearing screening.

26
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:36.290
A pass shows that outer hair cell activity can produce an emission through the

27
00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.150
middle ear. It does not prove intact hearing.

28
00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:44.450
In auditory neuropathy, an emission can be normal while the auditory brainstem

29
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.010
response is absent or very abnormal.

30
00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.390
The tests probe different stages.

31
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.400
In frogs, hair bundles can also add energy.

32
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.220
Martin and Hudspeth studied excised adult bullfrog saccular tissue.

33
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.440
A flexible probe moved individual bundles.

34
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.440
The bundles dissipated more energy through fluid drag than the probe supplied.

35
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.940
The extra energy came from the bundles themselves.

36
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.740
A large movement alone would not show that: a passive bundle can move far on energy

37
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:16.920
supplied by a probe.

38
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.240
This does not show what bundles contribute in mammals.

39
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.450
The brain also sends input back to outer hair cells.

40
00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.510
Medial olivocochlear efferents release acetylcholine.

41
00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.550
Calcium entry activates nearby potassium channels.

42
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:37.850
Acetylcholine shunts and hyperpolarizes the cells, suppressing their motility.

43
00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:43.430
This feedback regulates the cochlear amplifier, the active process that helps us

44
00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.430
detect weak sounds and separate nearby frequencies.
